.TH "ltsp-info" "1" "20260918" "Vagrant Cascadian" ""
.SH "NAME"
ltsp-info \(em display troubleshooting information about ltsp server and chroots
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBltsp-info\fR [\fB\-nv\fR] [\fB\-b\fR \fIdirectory\fR]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBltsp-info\fR will display information useful to troubleshooting issues on an
LTSP server: the server's distribution and release, the versions of the LTSP
packages installed on it, the chroots found under the base directory and their
package versions, the TFTP directories the clients boot from, the image files,
and every \fBlts.conf\fP(5) it finds.
.PP
The TFTP directories are reported in their own right, not once per chroot. The
client reads its \fBlts.conf\fP(5) and its kernel from there and from nowhere
else, so that listing matters whether or not a matching chroot still exists \(em
the chroot may have been built under a different base directory, or removed once
its image was in place. Which directories are looked at is set by
\fBTFTP_DIRS\fR and \fBTFTP_BOOT_DIR\fR, see \fBltsp-server.conf\fP(5); by
default that is \fI/var/lib/tftpboot/ltsp\fR, \fI/tftpboot/ltsp\fR and
\fI/srv/tftp/ltsp\fR.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.IP "\fB\-b\fP, \fB\-\-base\fP \fIdirectory\fR" 4
Look for chroots and images under \fIdirectory\fR instead of \fI/opt/ltsp\fR.
Use this when the chroot was built elsewhere, as
.br
\fBltsp\-build\-client \-\-base /srv/ltsp\fR
.br
does. The \fBBASE\fR environment variable does the same thing.
.IP "\fB\-n\fP, \fB\-\-no\-server\-info\fP" 4
Do not display server information.
.IP "\fB\-v\fP, \fB\-\-verbose\fP" 4
Display more information, such as including the contents of detected files.
.IP "\fB\-h\fP, \fB\-\-help\fP" 4
Display a short help message and exit.
.IP "\fB\-\-version\fP" 4
Output version information and exit.
.SH "EXAMPLE"
.PP
Report on the default base directory:
.PP
.RS 4
ltsp-info
.RE
.PP
Report on a build tree kept apart from the running one, with the contents of
every file found:
.PP
.RS 4
ltsp-info \-v \-b /srv/ltsp
.RE
.SH "NOTES"
.PP
\fBno chroot under /opt/ltsp\fR is not in itself a fault. A server that builds
with \fB\-\-base\fR, or that keeps only the image and deletes the chroot after
building, has none, and boots clients perfectly well. What matters in that case
is the TFTP listing and the image, both of which are reported regardless.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
\fBlts.conf\fP(5), \fBltsp-server.conf\fP(5), \fBltsp-build-client\fP(8),
\fBltsp-update-kernels\fP(8)
.SH "AVAILABILITY"
.PP
\fBltsp-info\fR is part of the \fBltsp\fP package
and the latest versions are available in source form from
\fIhttps://github.com/MAButz/ltsp6\fR.
